====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|physical}} To [[remove]] or [[block]] an [[opening]], [[gap]] or [[passage]] through.
## {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[move]] a thing, or part of a thing, nearer to another so that the [[gap]] or [[opening]] between the two is [[remove]]d.
##: {{syn|en|close up|shut}}
##: {{ant|en|open}}
##: {{ux|en|'''Close''' the door behind you when you leave.}}
##: {{ux|en|Many flowers '''close''' at night.}}
##: {{ux|en|Jim '''closed''' his eyes and reclined back in his chair.}}
##: {{ux|en|The runner in second place is '''closing''' the gap on the leader.}}
##: {{coi|en|to '''close''' the ranks of an army}}
##* {{RQ:Byron Childe Harold|canto=III|stanza=LXXXIV|passage=What deep wounds ever '''closed''' without a scar?}}
##* {{RQ:Christie Autobiography|II|2|passage=If I '''close''' my eyes I can see Marie today as I saw her then. Round, rosy face, snub nose, dark hair piled up in a chignon.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[obstruct]] or [[block]].
##: {{syn|en|close off|close up|shut|shut off}}
##: {{ant|en|open}}
##: {{ux|en|They '''closed''' the road for the festival.}}
##: {{ux|en|Ice has '''closed''' the channel to shipping.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become denser or more crowded with objects.
##: {{ux|en|As we penetrated further, the forest '''closed''' around us.}}
## {{lb|en|figuratively|transitive|intransitive}} To make or become [[unreceptive]].
##: {{ux|en|He has '''closed''' his mind to new ideas.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive|engineering|gas and liquid flow|of valve or damper}} To move to a position preventing [[fluid]] from flowing.
## {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive|electricity|of a switch, fuse or circuit breaker}} To move to a position allowing [[electricity]] to flow.
## To [[grapple]]; to engage in close [[combat]].
##* '''1856-1858''', {{w|William H. Prescott}}, ''History of the Reign of Phillip II''
##*: They boldly '''closed''' in a hand-to-hand contest.
## {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive|especially|sports}} To angle (a [[club]], [[bat]] or other hitting implement) [[downwards]] and/or  (for a [[right-hander]]) [[anticlockwise]] of straight.
# To finish.
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[end]] or [[conclude]].
##: {{syn|en|complete|end|finish|wind up|wrap up}}
##: {{ant|en|begin|commence|initiate|start}}
##: {{ux|en|The committee chairman made a few concluding remarks and then '''closed''' the session.}} 
##: {{ux|en|to '''close''' a bargain; &emsp; to '''close''' a course of instruction}}
##* {{RQ:Dryden Juvenal Satires|5|passage=One frugal supper did our studies '''close'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|XLVII|70|If these brief lays, of Sorrow born,<br>⁠Were taken to be such as '''closed'''<br>⁠Grave doubts and answers here proposed,<br>Then these were such as men might scorn: {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To finish; to come to an end.
##: {{syn|en|end|finish|wind up|wrap up}}
##: {{ant|en|begin|commence|start}}
##: {{ux|en|The debate '''closed''' at six o'clock.}}
## {{lb|en|ergative|marketing}} To [[conclude]] (a [[sale]]).
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To perform as the final act at (a show etc.).
##: {{ux|en|Nirvana '''closed''' the festival.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|baseball|pitching}} To make the final [[out]]s, usually three, of a game.
##: {{ux|en|He has '''closed''' the last two games for his team.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|finance}} To cancel or reverse (a trading position).
# To make or become non-operational or unavailable for use.
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To put out of use or operation.
##: {{syn|en|close down}}
##: {{ux|en|We are '''closing''' the phone lines at 9 pm.}}
##: {{ux|en|Your account has been '''closed''' because of non-payment.}}
##: {{ux|en|They '''closed''' the airport because of a bomb scare.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To cease operation or cease to be available.
##: {{ux|en|Phone lines will '''close''' in ten minutes.}}
##: {{ux|en|Our options are '''closing''' fast.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|of a [[business]], market etc.}} To [[cease]] [[trading]] for the day, or permanently.
##: {{syn|en|shut}}
##: {{ant|en|open}}
##: {{ux|en|The supermarket '''closes''' at eight o'clock.}}
##: {{ux|en|Lots of shops in the town centre have '''closed''' because of the recession.}}
##: {{ux|en|The FTSE 100 '''closed''' up 1.2%.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To do the tasks (putting things away, locking doors, etc.) required to prepare a store or other establishment to shut down for the night.
##: {{ux|en|Whoever '''closed''' last night forgot to turn off the closet light.}}
## {{lb|en|ergative|computing}} To terminate an [[application]], [[window]], [[file]] or [[database]] [[connection]], etc.
##: {{syn|en|exit<qq:an application>}}
##: {{ant|en|launch<qq:an application>|open|start<qq:an application>}}
##: {{ux|en|'''Close''' the file when you have finished reading data.}}
##: {{ux|en|This app has a bug: when you try to sort a large spreadsheet, it '''closes'''.}}
## {{lb|en|Philippines|Quebec}} To [[turn off]]; to [[switch off]].
##: {{ux|en|Please '''close''' the lights, the (electric) fan, the TV.}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|figurative}} To come or [[gather]] [[around]]; to [[enclose]].
#: {{syn|en|encompass|confine}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Jonah|2|5|passage=The depth '''closed''' me round about.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1633|author=w:George Herbert|title=The Church
|passage=But now Thou dost Thyself immure and '''close''' / In some one corner of a feeble heart; / Where yet both Sinne and Satan, Thy old foes, / Do pinch and straiten Thee, and use much art / To gain Thy thirds' and little part.}}
# {{lb|en|surveying}} To have a [[vector sum]] of 0; that is, to form a closed [[polygon]].

=====Usage notes=====
Due to the near-opposite meanings relating to fluid flow and electrical components, these usages are deprecated in safety-critical instructions, with the words ''to "on"'' or ''to "off"'' preferred, so instead of ''Close valve A; close switch B'' use ''Turn valve A to OFF; turn switch B to ON''.

