====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er}}

# Having little difference or distance in place, position, or abstractly; ''see also '''[[close to]]'''''.
## At little distance; [[near]] in space or time.
##: {{ux|en|Is your house '''close'''?}}
##: {{ux|en|Christmas is getting '''close'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=7|passage={{...}} St. Bede's at this period of its history was perhaps the poorest and most miserable parish in the East End of London. '''Close'''-packed, crushed by the buttressed height of the railway viaduct, rendered airless by huge walls of factories, it at once banished lively interest from a stranger's mind and left only a dull oppression of the spirit.}}
## Almost, but not quite (getting to an answer or goal); {{l|en|near}}.
##: {{ux|en|No, but you were '''close'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|We were so '''close''' to winning!}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-01|volume=407|issue=8838|page=71|magazine=w:The Economist| title=[http://www.economist.com/news/finance-and-economics/21578670-peer-peer-lending-needs-new-name-end-peer-show End of the peer show]| passage=Finance is seldom romantic. But the idea of peer-to-peer lending comes '''close'''. This is an industry that brings together individual savers and lenders on online platforms. Those that want to borrow are matched with those that want to lend.}}
## Nearly equal; almost evenly balanced or matched.
##: {{ux|en|Their ages are quite '''close'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|a '''close''' contest; a '''close''' fit}}
## Adhering strictly to a standard or original; exact.
##: {{ux|en|a '''close''' translation; a '''close''' copy}}
## Intimate in relationship.
##: {{ux|en|He is a '''close''' friend.}}
##: {{ux|en|My brother and I were '''close''' when we were younger, but not so much now.}}
# Compressed, restricted, constrained, etc.
## Narrow; confined.
##: {{ux|en|a '''close''' alley; '''close''' quarters; '''close''' confines}}
##* {{RQ:Dickens Pickwick Papers|41|text=a '''close''' prison}}
## {{lb|en|legal}} Of a corporation or other business entity, [[closely held]].
## Strictly confined; carefully guarded.
##: {{ux|en|a '''close''' prisoner}}
## Oppressive; without motion or ventilation; causing a feeling of [[lassitude]].
##* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|passage=If the rooms be low-roofed, or full of windows and doors, the one maketh the air '''close''', [...] and the other maketh it exceeding unequal.}}
##* '''1907''', {{w|Algernon Blackwood}}, [https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Listener_and_Other_Stories/The_Dance_of_Death The Dance of Death] [...]the artificial light and '''close''' air of his high office stool [...]
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1921|author=w:P. G. Wodehouse|title=w:Indiscretions of Archie|chapter=X| passage=He sighed drowsily. The atmosphere of the auction room was '''close'''; you weren't allowed to smoke; and altogether he was beginning to regret that he had come.}}
## {{lb|en|Ireland|UK|weather}} Hot, [[humid]], with no [[wind]].
## {{lb|en|archaic}} Dense; solid; compact.
##* {{RQ:Locke Human Understanding|text=The golden globe being put into a press, [...] the water made itself way through the pores of that very '''close''' metal.}}
# Accurate; careful; precise; also, attentive; undeviating; strict.
#: {{ux|en|The patient was kept under '''close''' observation.}}
#* {{RQ:Locke Conduct|page=90<!--|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=o0EVAAAAQAAJ&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&pg=PA90#v=onepage&q&f=false-->|text=I must acknowledge that hitherto I have discover’d no other way to keep our Thoughts '''cloſe''' to their Buſineſs, but the endeavouring as much as we can, and by frequent Attention and Application, getting the habit of Attention and Application.}}
# Short.
#: {{ux|en|to cut grass or hair '''close'''}}
# {{senseid|en|closed shut}}{{lb|en|now|rare}} [[closed|Closed]], [[shut]].
#* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|Matthew|8|text=There is nothinge so '''close''', that shall not be openned, and nothinge so hyd that shall not be knowen.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1830|author=w:Thomas Thomson (chemist)|title=The History of Chemistry|volume=1|pages=30–31
|passage=As the alchymists were assiduous workmen—as they mixed all the metals, salts, &c... and subjected such mixtures to the action of heat in '''close''' vessels, their labours were occasionally repaid by the discovery of new substances...}}
#* {{RQ:Charlotte Bronte Jane Eyre|chapter=1|text=I mounted into the window-seat: gathering up my feet, I sat cross-legged, like a Turk; and, having drawn the red [[moreen]] curtain nearly '''close''', I was [[shrine]]d in double retirement.}}
# {{senseid|en|linguistics}} {{lb|en|linguistics|phonetics|of a vowel}} Articulated with the tongue body relatively close to the hard palate.
# {{lb|en|heraldry|of a bird}} With its wings at its side, closed, held near to its body (typically also [[statant]]); {{lb|en|of wings}} in this posture. [[File:Complete Guide to Heraldry Fig446.png|right|thumb|An eagle close.]]
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1780|author=Joseph Edmondson|title=A Complete Body of Heraldry
|text=Crest, a cockatrice, wings '''close''', vert, combed and wattled gu.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1894|author=Henry Gough; James Parker|title=A Glossary of Terms Used in Heraldry|page=215
|text=Sable, an eagle '''close''' or - ROPER, Derby. / Sable, a chevron ermine between three eagles '''close''' argent - GAMES, Leicester, granted 1614. / Sable a chevron between three eagles '''close''' argent - JERVOISE.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1902|author=Lincoln's Inn (London, England)|title=The Records of the Honorable Society of Lincoln's Inn|page=458
|text=Arms : Azure, a chevron ermine between three cross - crosslets fitchy argent. Crest : An eagle '''close''' argent, ducally gorged.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} Difficult to obtain.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1886|work=United States Congressional Serial Set |title=Leases of Lands in the Indian Territory|author=|volume=2362|page=184|passage=Some of these parties have not paid their last payment, because money was '''close''' last fall.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1903| title=Gunton's Magazine of American Economics and Political Science|author=| page=249| passage=We are told out West that the reason money is so '''close''' now is because so large an amount has been invested in real estate. I cannot understand why that would make any difference if that money has been sent from one section of the country into another for the purpose of buying real estate. Why should it make any difference as to money being '''close'''? We are told in the East large amounts have been invested in the large manufacturing plants, such as the steel plants, etc. but if the money has been invested there it has simply changed hands, and why should that make any difference?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1965 | title=Country Life - Volume 137 |author=| page=326| passage=But there is reason underlying this confusion: time as well as money is '''close''' these days and a small wardrobe of hats can be very boring.}}
#: {{ux|en|Money is '''close'''.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} Parsimonious; stingy.
#* {{RQ:Keats Lamia|poem=Isabella|stanza=XVII|page=57|passage=Yet were these Florentines as self-retired / In hungry pride and gainful cowardice, / As two '''close''' Hebrews in that land inspired, / Paled in and vineyarded from beggar-spies; [...]}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1837|author=w:Nathaniel Hawthorne|title=Twice-Told Tales|volume=I|chapter=Mr. Higginbotham's Catastrophe
|passage=[...] he was a crusty old fellow, as '''close''' as a vice.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Bleak House|passage=Though a hard-grained man, '''close''', dry, and silent, he can enjoy old wine with the best. He has a priceless bin of port in some artful cellar under the Fields, which is one of his many secrets.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Out of the way of observation; secluded; secret; hidden.
#* {{RQ:KJV|1 Chronicles|12|1|text=He yet kept himself '''close''' because of Saul.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=III|canto=I|stanza=57|passage=her '''close''' intent}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} Concise; to the point.
#: {{ux|en|'''close''' reasoning}}
#* '''1690''', {{w|John Dryden}}, ''Translations'' (Preface)
#*: Where the original is '''close''' no version can reach it in the same compass.
# Marked, evident.{{rfex|en}}

